WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.330
SALVATORE SFERRAZZA:
Hey, everyone.

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.390
In this video, I'll talk about
the challenges of analyzing

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.370
market data in a
traditional environment

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.520
and how CME Smart Stream
with Google Cloud could help.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.732
Risk is defined as a situation
involving exposure to danger.

00:00:12.732 --> 00:00:14.190
When it comes to
the topic of risk,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.770
trading firms are inherently
sensitive, especially

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.710
in current times where global
systems are all being impacted

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.790
from market volatility.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:23.520
They manage risk by
collecting signals

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.900
from a diverse
set of data points

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.272
and interpreting them to make
timely business decisions.

00:00:28.272 --> 00:00:29.730
One key source of
these data points

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.497
is CME Smart Stream, which
delivers a real-time feed

00:00:32.497 --> 00:00:34.080
of financial and
commodity market data

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.470
that traders can use as an input
to inform a risk management

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.160
strategy.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.250
First, an introduction
to CME Group.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.850
CME Group runs exchanges where
futures contracts are traded.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.680
For example, a corn
futures contract

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.610
guarantees a farmer
that at harvest time,

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.450
they'll get a certain amount
of money for their corn,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.930
and guarantees a
processing plant

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.610
that they'll get a certain
amount of corn for their money.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:57.665
The contract price is
the price of certainty.

00:00:57.665 --> 00:00:59.040
These contracts
work the same way

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.500
for corn, oil, or even stock.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.383
To get indications of
risk, trading firms

00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:04.800
working for the
parties constantly

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.780
monitor live contract prices.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.550
To do so, exchanges
traditionally

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.530
send each change in
a contract's price

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.350
to a trading firm's data center,
requiring significant resources

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.200
for data storage and analytics.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.420
With CME Smart Stream,
that same price data

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.650
is delivered in real time to
trading firms' Cloud resources.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.150
We selected three
personas that care

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.370
about the firm's overall risk--

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:29.140
a risk manager, an
infrastructure engineer,

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:30.930
and a data scientist.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.360
Let's take a look at each role.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.120
First, the risk manager.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.950
Volatility and
information asymmetry

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.950
injects uncertainty into
the firm's decision process.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.875
Timely standardized
market data can help,

00:01:42.875 --> 00:01:45.000
but the traditional methods
for receiving such data

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.880
are oftentimes
costly and complex.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.130
In contrast, Google
Cloud attaches directly

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.770
to CME Smart Stream to store
large data sets in our BigQuery

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.740
backend and allow analysis
through a plethora of options,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.770
including standard SQL.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:59.730
When combined, risk
managers easily

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.040
get the visualizations they
need while paying for only

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.013
the resources they use.

00:02:04.013 --> 00:02:06.180
And as a result, there are
afforded a real-time view

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.910
of exposure that can help them
adjust positions and mitigate

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.210
risk promptly when necessary.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:15.210
Next, let's take a look at
the data scientist's world.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.850
For trading firms, this area
is riddled with complexity.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.940
You've got to acquire, ingest,
explore, and analyze the data

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.940
all before you can take action.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.200
But Google Cloud
allows data scientists

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.820
to retrieve CME
Smart Stream data,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.830
transform it via data
flow, and store it

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.080
in Bigtable or BigQuery.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.810
Then, the data scientist can use
Google Cloud's AutoML platform

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.470
to train and deploy models
that predict whether a contract

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.540
price will move up or down.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.350
As you can see, she could
store, analyze, and visualize

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.100
the results with tools
that scale, deploy,

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.047
and adapt in real time.

00:02:46.047 --> 00:02:48.630
Finally, let's look at this from
the infrastructure engineer's

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.070
perspective.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:51.840
One of the big problems
many firms face

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.640
is that inbound
financial data traverses

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.080
the public internet or a costly
private line before ingestion.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.180
As an alternative,
Google Cloud offers

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.540
managed networking functionality
regionally and globally.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.310
This way, the
infrastructure engineer

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.560
can connect the on-prem
or remote resources

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.500
to Google Cloud without needing
to replicate connectivity

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.590
or administrative
policies in each region.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.300
Additionally, training
firms struggle

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.080
with keeping on-prem or even
Cloud resources up and running.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.180
As our demo shows,
this problem could be

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:24.360
managed simply and effectively.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.310
We start by leveraging
terraform descriptive

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:29.372
deployment of our
microservices architecture.

00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:31.830
This architecture allows us to
containerize our application

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.510
components, while
making it easier

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.910
to support CI/CD pipelines.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.280
Terraform enables the
automation of infrastructure

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.080
making infrastructure
easily auditable

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.750
for any governance need.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:44.070
And that's it.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.950
In this demo, we showed you how
risk managers, data scientists,

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.720
and infrastructure
engineers can easily

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.770
manage risk in the Cloud
using real-time market data.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:53.725
We hope you found it useful.

00:03:53.725 --> 00:03:55.600
For more about everything
we've talked about,

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.565
check out the links in
the description below.

00:03:57.565 --> 00:03:59.940
And don't forget to subscribe
to our Google Cloud YouTube

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.540
channel for more great content.

